{
  "recorded_on": "2026-08-03",
  "dark_baseline": 1,
  "how_this_file_is_read": "Every `deferred_*` array below is the pinned SET of test binaries that execute nowhere. check-executed-tests.mjs compares that set against what it measures and fails on EITHER difference: a binary that darkens and is not named here, and a binary named here that now executes or disappears. dark_baseline is derived from that named union and must agree with it; it is a cross-check, never the ratchet itself. A count alone passed silently on the most likely shape a wiring refactor takes — darken one binary, wire another, net zero — and this repository has already paid for that exact defect once, on the personal-data baseline, whose NET COUNT pin took six rounds to correct. `defined_feature_variants` is a different thing and is NOT part of the dark set; see its own note.",
  "history": "Was 287 on 2026-07-30, then 10 on 2026-07-31 after 177 previously-dark PostgreSQL targets began to execute and pass, later 13 when the measured binary population expanded, and back to 10 on 2026-08-03 after the three shared-fixture binaries were repaired, executed, and wired. Remaining fixture/defect entries are wired-capable but fail on first execution, so those failures are fixture rot or defects the silence hid — none is a regression. Separately, deferred_out_of_pivot tracks an ignored service-backed binary excluded by the current product pivot.",
  "why_10_became_11": "NOT a regression and NOT a comparable number: the population changed. check-executed-tests.mjs counted FILES and now counts TEST BINARIES — (crate_root, feature set) — because a file alone cannot distinguish //backend/app:console-app-unit from //backend/app:console-app-itest-inline-postgres, which share backend/app/src/lib.rs and differ only in `features = [\"test-postgres\"]`. They are not variants of one thing: `cargo test -p console-app --lib` lists 152 tests and `--lib --features test-postgres` lists 17, because each compiles the other's `cfg(not(...))` half out. Under the old file key, deleting either wrapper from ci.yml moved no number at all. The finer key adds exactly two entries repo-wide (the two crate roots with a second, feature-carrying rust_test) and one of them was already dark, below.",
  "why_11_became_12": "The population changed again, in the FILE dimension this time. A crate's lib test binary was admitted only when `src/lib.rs` ITSELF contained `#[cfg(test)]`. Nine crates keep every one of theirs in `src/<module>.rs`, so their lib binaries were never in the measured population, could never be reported dark, and could be unwired with no observable effect — `backend/crates/kernel/core/src/lib.rs` has zero `#[cfg(test)]`, and deleting `-p console-kernel-core` from ci.yml (reverting #559, which landed for exactly this) moved no number at all. Admitting `#[cfg(test)]` anywhere under `src/` took defined 319 -> 328. Of the 9 newly visible binaries, 2 were already wired (kernel/core, payroll/adapter-postgres) and 6 were wired in the same change — 61 pure tests, listed in ci.yml's comment. The 7th, platform/db, is below.",
  "deferred_needs_postgres_lane": [],
  "why_platform_db_lib_is_here": "All 9 of its unit tests are `#[sqlx::test]` (`cargo test -p console-platform-db --lib -- --list` names them; every one is under `audit_tx::tests`, and they are the append-only audit guards — UPDATE and DELETE rejection, the rehome guard, and transactional atomicity). They panic in sqlx's harness without a database, so they cannot join the `backend` job's --lib step with the other six. Wiring them needs a PostgreSQL lane, which is Buck2-exit work, not this change.",
  "deferred_feature_variant": [],
  "why_auth_rest_lib_is_here": "The no-features unit binary of console-platform-auth-rest is named in no workflow step: ci.yml runs only //tools/buck:auth-rest-dev-auth-* , which is `--features dev-auth`. Executed here: `cargo test --lib -p console-platform-auth-rest` lists 6 tests, `--features dev-auth` lists 7, so this crate's plain binary IS a strict subset of one that runs, and no test of it is currently unexecuted. It stays counted because subsumption is a property of today's source, not of the wiring — backend/app is the same shape with the opposite answer — and this gate deliberately does not model it. Wiring it needs a PostgreSQL lane (2 of the 6 are #[sqlx::test] and panic on a missing DATABASE_URL), which is the Buck2-exit work, not this change.",
  "deferred_fixture_or_defect": [],
  "why_13_became_10": "The generated Buck faces now expose backend/test_support through explicit public file targets and map the dispatch/mobile shared fixtures into all four affected integration binaries. A local disposable-PostgreSQL run passed timer_delivery (3 tests), mobile_device_registration (3), mobile_evidence (4), and mobile_sync (5). The three formerly dark binaries are now exact required targets in the serialized PostgreSQL CI lane; mobile_device_registration was already required and is recorded here only as part of the repair evidence.",
  "deferred_out_of_pivot": [
    "backend/crates/platform/storage/tests/seaweedfs_worm.rs"
  ],
  "why_seaweedfs_worm_is_dark": "This ignored integration test requires a live SeaweedFS service. The former plain cargo invocation selected zero tests and therefore was not coverage. The current Console pivot explicitly excludes evidence/WORM and field-operations work, so adding a new service-backed CI lane here would expand scope. The binary remains named in the exact dark set so it cannot be mistaken for executed coverage or disappear silently.",
  "why_production_lifecycle_is_here": "Two defects in it were already fixed in this branch — a 52-character organizations slug against a 40-character ceiling, and a missing ont_object_type_key_revisions sidecar parent. It now fails 23514, a third and different CHECK violation.",
  "defined_feature_variants": [
    "backend/app/src/lib.rs --features test-postgres",
    "backend/crates/platform/auth-rest/src/lib.rs --features dev-auth"
  ],
  "why_feature_variants_are_pinned_here": "These are NOT dark and are NOT part of the set above — they execute today. They are pinned into the DEFINED population because both of the other sources for a binary's feature dimension are artifacts that a deletion PR deletes. `rust_test features` was the only source, so a commit removing //tools/buck:auth-rest-dev-auth-inline-postgres from ci.yml together with its sh_test and its rust_test — the ordinary shape of a wrapper-removal PR — dropped this binary from `defined` and from `executed` in the same instant. The dark set did not move, the gate exited 0, and an entire dev-auth PostgreSQL suite stopped running with no diagnostic. Pinned, the binary survives its own deletion, goes dark, and names itself. Two entries because repo-wide there are exactly two crate roots carrying a second, feature-bearing binary; a pinned entry that stops being `defined` is reported UNRESOLVED, so this list cannot rot into decoration.",
  "test_attribute_baseline_note": "This map is a cheap lexical deletion ratchet over reachable source files, not a runtime case count. It counts declared #[test], #[tokio::test], and #[sqlx::test] attributes without evaluating cfg expressions, feature selection, macro expansion, or #[ignore]. A source reached by multiple feature-bearing binaries appears once; exact (source, feature set) binary reachability is enforced separately by the named dark-set ratchet. For example, console-app's bare and test-postgres binaries list 153 and 18 tests respectively in the compiler harness, while this static source metric records 171 attributes once.",
